  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "How to read the alternatives",
      "items": [
        "A and B form the normal request/completion path only when fresh admission succeeds. Confirmed resource pressure exits A without a task record.",
        "C is a separate active-task cancellation path, not a step after B has published a terminal outcome. D is an explicit new request after the predecessor ends."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "The result has one owner",
      "items": [
        "The runner closes the task lifetime, seals provider output and asks TaskDir to finalize. TaskDir interprets that sealed evidence and commits outcome.json.",
        "A response carrying a task id alone does not establish task creation. Observe only a task_record: created response and follow its failure guidance.",
        "Collection may reconcile existing saved evidence; it does not launch another provider turn (internal/tasklifecycle/collection.go:13–63)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Cancellation and continuation",
      "items": [
        "The active guardian handles native termination. If the task is idle, stopping the saved queue row requires its lifetime-owner lease; an acknowledgement alone does not prove closure (internal/tasklifecycle/stop.go:14–82).",
        "The new continuation task uses the saved provider session in the caller's current scope. A missing or incompatible session refuses continuation; it does not invent one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Optional Git isolation",
      "items": [
        "This sequence shows the ordinary provider turn. Managed workspace preparation and capture are optional, with their own receipts (internal/workspaces/admission.go:16–62; capture.go:123–150).",
        "Workspace capture requires its helper lifetime to close and remains independent of the task's terminal verdict."
      ]
    }
  ]
